<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ws-04-calibration">
  <title>Worksheet 4: Calibration and Adjudication</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Complete a full coding pass independently under the same frozen materials as another coder.</li>
      <li>Preserve both independent records before any comparison begins.</li>
      <li>Locate disagreements at the earliest workflow decision where the records diverge.</li>
      <li>Adjudicate from visible evidence and governing rules without forcing agreement.</li>
      <li>Use calibration evidence to assess readiness and identify needed remediation or artifact revision.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This worksheet is the fourth milestone in the researcher training sequence.
      Calibration tests whether researchers can apply the same frozen framework,
      protocol, codebook, vocabulary, and question-specific materials independently.
      Agreement reached only after discussion is not evidence of independent
      reproducibility. Each coder must first complete and lock a full record.
    </p>

    <p>
      Calibration is development work. Its purpose is to diagnose where the human
      method is stable and where coders, examples, code definitions, question frames,
      or protocol rules require further work. The assigned calibration responses must
      remain separate from the held-out validation corpus.
    </p>

    <p>
      Comparison begins only after the research lead confirms that all independent
      records are complete and preserved. During comparison, disagreement is treated
      as evidence. Coders identify the earliest divergent decision, state the support
      for each reading, and resolve the case only when one interpretation is better
      supported by the response and current rules.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-ws-04-status">
      <title>Milestone status</title>
      <p>
        Completing a calibration packet does not automatically authorize work with
        project responses. The research lead must apply the readiness rule recorded in
        the packet, review the pattern of disagreements, and document an advancement,
        remediation, repetition, or materials-revision decision.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-overview">
    <title>Milestone at a Glance</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-04-overview">
      <title>Purpose and output of the calibration milestone.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="22%"/>
        <col width="78%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Item</cell>
          <cell>Description</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Primary purpose</cell>
          <cell>Assess independent reproducibility of the complete human coding method and diagnose the earliest source of disagreement.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Work mode</cell>
          <cell>Independent full coding, followed by authorized comparison and adjudication.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Data</cell>
          <cell>A shared calibration set that is excluded from held-out validation.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Governing materials</cell>
          <cell>The same frozen question frames, protocol, codebook, vocabulary, handbook, and schema for every coder.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Primary deliverables</cell>
          <cell>Locked independent records, a field-level comparison, disagreement records, adjudicated or unresolved results, and a readiness review.</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Next milestone</cell>
          <cell>Supervised coding of approved project responses, after the research lead documents readiness.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-prerequisites">
    <title>Prerequisites and Assigned Reading</title>

    <p>
      Begin only after the research lead has accepted Worksheet 3 and supplied a
      versioned calibration packet. Review the following material before opening the
      response set:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-practice-calibration" text="title"/> and
          <xref ref="sec-practice-readiness" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-practice-calibration" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-disagreement-types" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-disagreement-notes" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-disagreement-adjudication" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="subsec-disagreement-unresolved" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The ten steps in <xref ref="sec-coding-pass" text="title"/> and the
          preservation rule in <xref ref="sec-coding-sheet" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-study-calibration" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-study-adjudication" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="subsec-study-human-ceiling" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-study-design" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-app-coding-sheet-response-fields" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-app-coding-sheet-episode-fields" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-app-coding-sheet-analytic-fields" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="subsec-app-coding-sheet-provenance-fields" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-workflow" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-review" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-provenance" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="app-protocol-summary" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-data-records-preserve" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-data-version-discipline" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="subsec-data-work-sessions" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-data-management" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-04-examples">
      <title>Do not consult solved examples during the independent phase</title>
      <p>
        The worked-example chapter may be used later for authorized remediation. Do not
        search it for a response that resembles an item in the calibration packet unless
        the packet explicitly permits that access.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-phases">
    <title>Access Rules by Phase</title>

    <p>
      Calibration has three phases. The research lead controls the transition between
      them. Do not begin a later phase merely because your own record is complete.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-04-phases">
      <title>Materials available during each calibration phase.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="20%"/>
        <col width="40%"/>
        <col width="40%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Phase</cell>
          <cell>You may use</cell>
          <cell>You must not use</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Independent coding</cell>
          <cell>The assigned response packet, frozen handbook, protocol, codebook, vocabulary, question frames, normative references, and coding schema.</cell>
          <cell>Another coder's work, adjudicated results, comparison summaries, machine proposals, or response-specific advice.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Comparison</cell>
          <cell>All locked independent records for the assigned coder pair or group, the disagreement taxonomy, and the governing materials.</cell>
          <cell>Silent edits to either independent record or a machine-generated answer used to settle the disagreement.</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Adjudication</cell>
          <cell>The original question and response, all preserved coder records, the governing materials, and the authorized adjudicator or research lead.</cell>
          <cell>Compromise without evidence, undocumented policy changes, or replacement of the original records.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-04-transition">
      <title>Wait for the comparison release</title>
      <p>
        The research lead must confirm that every required independent record has been
        locked before coder identities or annotations are revealed. Early comparison
        compromises the calibration evidence for all affected responses.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-packet">
    <title>Check the Calibration Packet</title>

    <p>
      Before coding, confirm that the packet defines the corpus, shared resources,
      comparison plan, and readiness rule. Record the supplied values below. Do not
      choose a readiness threshold after seeing the results.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-04-packet-manifest">
      <title>Calibration packet manifest.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="34%"/>
        <col width="66%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Required item</cell>
          <cell>Value or confirmation</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>Calibration batch ID</cell><cell>Record the stable identifier supplied for the shared set.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Corpus role</cell><cell>Confirm that the set is labeled calibration or development and excluded from held-out validation.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Coder assignments</cell><cell>Record the approved coder IDs and comparison grouping.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Question and response IDs</cell><cell>Confirm complete, stable identifiers and identical assignments across coders.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Question materials</cell><cell>Record each question-frame and normative-reference version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Analytic materials</cell><cell>Record the handbook, protocol, codebook, and vocabulary versions.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Schema and comparison form</cell><cell>Record the coding-sheet, comparison-sheet, and adjudication-record versions.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Readiness rule</cell><cell>Record the pre-specified quantitative and qualitative evidence the research lead will use. If no numerical threshold is adopted, state that explicitly.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Agreement summaries</cell><cell>Record which decisions will receive pre-adjudication agreement summaries and who will calculate them.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Work-session requirements</cell><cell>Record required session fields, sitting limits, and submission deadlines.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Approved project location</cell><cell>Confirm where independent, comparison, and adjudication records must be stored.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-04-stop-packet">
      <title>Stop when shared materials differ</title>
      <p>
        Stop and contact the research lead if coders received different question
        contexts, codebook versions, schemas, response sets, or readiness instructions.
        Calibration cannot measure reproducibility when the governing conditions differ.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-record-layers">
    <title>Records to Preserve</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-04-record-layers">
      <title>Calibration record layers and their roles.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="23%"/>
        <col width="31%"/>
        <col width="46%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Layer</cell>
          <cell>Created when</cell>
          <cell>Preservation rule</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Independent coder record</cell>
          <cell>Before any comparison</cell>
          <cell>One immutable full coding record per coder, including confidence, notes, and governing versions.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Comparison record</cell>
          <cell>After all independent records are locked</cell>
          <cell>Aligns corresponding decisions and marks agreement, difference, or non-comparability without changing either source record.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Disagreement record</cell>
          <cell>For every substantive divergence</cell>
          <cell>Records the earliest disagreement type, each reading, visible evidence, and governing reference.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Adjudication record</cell>
          <cell>After evidence-based review</cell>
          <cell>Stores the resolved decision or explicit unresolved status and the rationale. It does not replace coder records.</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Follow-up proposal</cell>
          <cell>When calibration exposes a recurring weakness</cell>
          <cell>Records proposed training, example, vocabulary, codebook, question-frame, or policy changes for versioned review.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-fields">
    <title>Fields for This Milestone</title>

    <p>
      Use the assigned schema and field mapping. The independent record contains the
      complete human coding workflow. Comparison and adjudication are stored in new
      layers rather than inserted into the locked coder records.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-04-fields">
      <title>Fields completed during calibration.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="30%"/>
        <col width="18%"/>
        <col width="52%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Field or record group</cell>
          <cell>Action</cell>
          <cell>Calibration rule</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Response, episode, analytic, confidence, and provenance fields</cell>
          <cell>Complete independently</cell>
          <cell>Each coder completes all fields authorized by the frozen protocol without consulting another record.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Session log</cell>
          <cell>Complete independently</cell>
          <cell>Record the required start, stop, interruption, or fatigue information specified in the packet.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Field alignment</cell>
          <cell>Complete during comparison</cell>
          <cell>Map corresponding response- and episode-level decisions. Preserve non-alignment when episode structures differ.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>disagreement_type</c></cell>
          <cell>Complete when records diverge</cell>
          <cell>Assign the earliest applicable taxonomy value. Record later consequences separately when useful.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Coder evidence and alternatives</cell>
          <cell>Complete when records diverge</cell>
          <cell>Preserve each original value and rationale, then add the exact response or question evidence supporting each reading.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>adjudication_status</c></cell>
          <cell>Complete after review</cell>
          <cell>Use the assigned value for resolved, unresolved, pending, or not required.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Adjudicated value and rationale</cell>
          <cell>Complete when resolved</cell>
          <cell>State the supported result and the governing rule. Do not enter a consensus value without a rationale.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Follow-up action</cell>
          <cell>Complete when needed</cell>
          <cell>Record no change, targeted practice, example revision, vocabulary proposal, codebook clarification, question-frame correction, policy proposal, or planned recoding.</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Machine proposal and tool-review fields</cell>
          <cell>Leave blank</cell>
          <cell>Calibration of the human method must remain separate from later workflow–human comparison unless the study protocol defines a distinct condition.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-procedure">
    <title>Calibration Procedure</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Verify the packet.</em> Confirm shared question contexts, response IDs,
          frozen versions, coder assignments, readiness rules, and approved storage.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Complete the independent pass.</em> Apply all ten workflow steps to every
          assigned response. Record uncertainty rather than requesting another coder's
          interpretation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Lock each independent record.</em> Submit through the approved project
          location and preserve the coder ID, timestamp, versions, and work-session log.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Release comparison only after all records are locked.</em> The research
          lead confirms procedural integrity before revealing paired records.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Compare field by field.</em> Begin with screening and episode structure,
          then compare components, relations, matches, correctness, coverage, status,
          disposition, confidence, and notes. Do not compare final codes alone.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Identify the earliest divergence.</em> Use the disagreement taxonomy.
          Later differences may be consequences of an earlier boundary or component
          disagreement.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Inspect low-confidence agreements.</em> Agreement does not guarantee that
          the shared decision is supported. Flag cases in which coders used the same
          unsupported assumption or reported substantial uncertainty.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Preserve pre-adjudication summaries.</em> Calculate or submit the records
          needed for the pre-specified agreement summaries before any value is changed
          through adjudication.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Adjudicate from evidence.</em> Return to the exact question and response,
          state the evidence for each interpretation, consult the frozen materials, and
          adopt a result only when one reading is better supported.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Preserve unresolved cases.</em> Record the competing interpretations, the
          evidence for each, and the rule or information that is missing.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Classify follow-up.</em> Distinguish individual remediation from a shared
          artifact problem. Do not create an immediate one-case exception during the
          calibration meeting.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Complete the readiness record.</em> Summarize demonstrated competencies,
          recurring difficulties, unresolved limitations, and the research lead's
          advancement decision.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-assignment">
    <title>Assignment</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-04-manifest">
      <title>Part A: Complete the Packet Manifest</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Record every required packet item and version before opening the first
          response. Report any mismatch rather than substituting a local copy.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-04-independent">
      <title>Part B: Complete and Lock the Independent Coding</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Code every assigned response using the full workflow and assigned schema.
          Preserve complete and incomplete episodes, alternatives, low confidence,
          OOV, no match, unresolved decisions, and all governing versions. Submit and
          lock the record before comparison.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-04-align">
      <title>Part C: Align the Independent Records</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          After the research lead releases the records, align them by
          <c>response_id</c> and corresponding episode decisions. When episode counts or
          boundaries differ, preserve the mismatch rather than forcing a one-to-one row
          pairing.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-04-disagreements">
      <title>Part D: Classify Disagreements</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each substantive difference:
          <ol>
            <li>identify the earliest workflow stage at which the records diverge;</li>
            <li>assign the applicable disagreement type;</li>
            <li>copy each coder's original value and rationale;</li>
            <li>state the visible evidence for each reading; and</li>
            <li>identify any later fields affected by the earlier difference.</li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-04-agreements">
      <title>Part E: Review Low-Confidence Agreements</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Inspect every agreement involving confidence 1, an unresolved note, a policy
          concern, or an unusual inference. Record whether the agreement appears
          independently well supported or whether both coders may have made the same
          unsupported assumption.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-04-summary">
      <title>Part F: Preserve the Pre-Adjudication Summary</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Submit the locked comparison needed for the pre-specified agreement analyses.
          Do not begin adjudication until the research lead confirms that the
          pre-adjudication record has been preserved.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-04-adjudicate">
      <title>Part G: Adjudicate or Preserve as Unresolved</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each disagreement, apply the adjudication procedure in
          <xref ref="subsec-disagreement-adjudication" text="title"/>. Record a resolved
          result only when the evidence and frozen rules support it. Otherwise, record
          <c>unresolved</c> and state what prevents a defensible final decision.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-04-follow-up">
      <title>Part H: Classify Follow-Up Needs</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For every recurring difficulty, determine whether the evidence supports:
          <ul>
            <li>no change;</li>
            <li>targeted coder remediation;</li>
            <li>a new or revised worked example;</li>
            <li>a vocabulary proposal;</li>
            <li>a codebook clarification;</li>
            <li>a question-frame or normative-reference correction;</li>
            <li>a protocol or retention-policy proposal; or</li>
            <li>planned recoding after a versioned change.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-04-readiness">
      <title>Part I: Complete the Readiness Evidence Record</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using <xref ref="tab-practice-readiness" text="title"/>, record evidence for
          each competency, the principal remaining difficulties, any limitations on
          future authorization, and the research lead's decision. Do not reduce the
          decision to one agreement statistic unless the packet explicitly adopts that
          rule.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-questions">
    <title>Questions, Conflicts, and Incidents</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-04-questions">
      <title>Required action when calibration conditions are threatened.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="33%"/>
        <col width="67%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Situation</cell>
          <cell>Required action</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>You receive a different packet or version from another coder</cell><cell>Stop and notify the research lead. Do not harmonize materials privately.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>You see another coder's work before locking your record</cell><cell>Stop, preserve the work completed before exposure, and report the incident. The research lead determines whether affected responses must be replaced.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Episode structures cannot be aligned one to one</cell><cell>Record a <c>BOUNDARY</c> or related disagreement and compare the competing structures without forcing row alignment.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Both coders agree but used the same unsupported assumption</cell><cell>Flag a low-confidence or policy concern. Agreement alone does not validate the decision.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>A codebook or protocol flaw becomes apparent</cell><cell>Preserve the current-version coding, record the artifact problem, and create a follow-up proposal. Do not revise the rule during the active comparison.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The response remains ambiguous after review</cell><cell>Preserve the alternatives and record an unresolved adjudication status.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>A privacy, access, or identifier problem appears</cell><cell>Stop the affected work and follow the incident or data-quality procedure in <xref ref="ch-data-management" text="title"/>.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>The readiness rule is missing or changed after results are viewed</cell><cell>Do not issue an advancement decision until the research lead documents the rule and the effect of the change.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-deliverables">
    <title>Deliverables</title>

    <p>
      Submit the following through the approved project location:
      <ol>
        <li>the completed calibration packet manifest;</li>
        <li>one locked independent full-coding record for each assigned coder;</li>
        <li>the work-session logs required by the packet;</li>
        <li>the field-level comparison record;</li>
        <li>the preserved pre-adjudication agreement input or summary;</li>
        <li>one disagreement record for every substantive divergence;</li>
        <li>the adjudicated result or explicit unresolved status for each disagreement;</li>
        <li>the follow-up and artifact-revision proposals;</li>
        <li>the competency-based readiness record; and</li>
        <li>the version record below.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-04-version-record">
      <title>Version information to preserve with the calibration submission.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="35%"/>
        <col width="65%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Field</cell><cell>Entry</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>worksheet_version</c></cell><cell><c>ws-04-calibration-v0.1</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>pass_type</c></cell><cell><c>calibration</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>batch_id</c></cell><cell>Record the calibration batch identifier.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>coder_ids</c></cell><cell>Record the approved project identifiers for the compared coders.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>handbook_version</c></cell><cell>Record the assigned version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>protocol_version</c></cell><cell>Record the assigned version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>codebook_version</c></cell><cell>Record the assigned frozen version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>vocabulary_version</c></cell><cell>Record the assigned frozen version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>question_frame_version</c></cell><cell>Record every version represented in the batch.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>comparison_schema_version</c></cell><cell>Record the assigned comparison and adjudication schema.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><c>completed_at</c></cell><cell>Record the final submission date.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-checklist">
    <title>Completion Checklist</title>

    <p>
      Before submitting, confirm that:
      <ul>
        <li>every coder used the same frozen response set and governing materials;</li>
        <li>all independent records were complete and locked before comparison;</li>
        <li>no coder consulted another record or machine proposal during the independent phase;</li>
        <li>comparison began with screening and episode structure rather than final codes;</li>
        <li>every disagreement is classified at its earliest divergent decision;</li>
        <li>each reading is supported by exact response, question, or governing-rule evidence;</li>
        <li>pre-adjudication information was preserved before resolution;</li>
        <li>unresolved cases remain visible;</li>
        <li>artifact proposals do not silently change current-version records;</li>
        <li>readiness evidence addresses the full competency model; and</li>
        <li>all files remain in the approved project location without identifying information in notes.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-04-review">
    <title>Review and Advancement</title>

    <p>
      The research lead first reviews procedural integrity: identical assigned
      materials, preserved independence, complete records, correct versions, and a
      locked pre-adjudication comparison. Analytic review then examines disagreement
      patterns, low-confidence agreements, adjudication quality, unresolved cases, and
      evidence across the readiness competencies in
      <xref ref="tab-practice-readiness" text="title"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Possible outcomes are:
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li>
          <title>Advance to supervised coding</title>
          <p>The calibration evidence demonstrates independent, defensible use of the complete method under the pre-specified readiness rule.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Targeted remediation</title>
          <p>Complete a new focused set addressing the earliest recurring difficulty, then append the remediation evidence to the readiness record.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Repeat calibration</title>
          <p>Complete a fresh calibration set when independence was compromised, disagreements remain broadly unstable, or the first set was not sufficiently diagnostic.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Resolve a materials problem</title>
          <p>Revise and version a defective question frame, codebook entry, vocabulary item, example, schema, or protocol rule, then determine which cases require recoding.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Preserve a limited authorization</title>
          <p>Advance only for specified question families or decision types while requiring secondary review for documented limitations.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      The advancement record must state the evidence used, the governing versions,
      remaining limitations, any required secondary review, and the date of the
      decision. Original calibration, comparison, and adjudication records remain
      unchanged.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
